    "content": "The Artemis program’s Space Launch System (SLS) rocket is built by several major aerospace contractors: Boeing builds the core stage, Northrop Grumman provides the solid rocket boosters, and Aerojet Rocketdyne (an L3Harris company) produces the RS-25 engines. The Orion spacecraft itself is built by Lockheed Martin. Key manufacturers for the Artemis missions include: Boeing: Produces the 212-foot core stage, which houses the main engines and flight systems. Northrop Grumman: Manufactures the twin five-segment solid rocket boosters, which provide 75% of the thrust at launch. Aerojet Rocketdyne: Supplies the RS-25 engines and other propulsion systems. Airbus: Builds the European Service Module (ESM) for the Orion capsule for the European Space Agency. United Launch Alliance (ULA): Developed the Interim Cryogenic Propulsion Stage (ICPS). The Artemis II crew's journey in space has begun. This video explains the history and components of the Space Launch System:47sVideoFromSpaceYouTube• Mar 30, 2026 47s The SLS is managed by NASA, with different components manufactured across the U.S. and in Europe.",
